const C={
c:{name:"C Programming: Zero to Systems Foundations",intro:"Learn how a C program is compiled and executed, then master procedural programming, memory and structured data.",modules:[
["Your first C program","C is a compiled, general-purpose language used in systems, embedded software and performance-sensitive applications. A C source file is compiled into a program. A typical toolchain uses a compiler such as GCC or Clang.","#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"Hello, World!\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","stdio.h declares standard input/output functions. main is the program entry point. printf prints text; return 0 signals successful completion.","Write a program that prints your name, course and three learning goals on separate lines.","What is the usual role of a C compiler?","Translate source code into an executable program",["Display web pages","Store files only"],0],
["Types, variables & operators","C is statically typed: declare a variable with a type before using it. Common types include int, char, float and double. Use meaningful names and initialise variables. sizeof reports storage size in bytes.","int age = 21;\ndouble price = 49.50;\nchar grade = 'A';\nprintf(\"%d %.2f %c\\n\", age, price, grade);","Format specifiers must match the argument types: %d for int, %f for floating-point output (printf promotes float to double), and %c for char. Use %zu for sizeof results.","Build a small bill calculator with item price, quantity and total. Use double for money-like example values and explain why production finance software needs decimal-safe representations.","Which type is commonly used for a single character?","char",["int","double"],0],
["Input, decisions & loops","scanf can read formatted input, but always check its return value. if/else makes decisions. for, while and do-while repeat work. C uses braces to define blocks and semicolons to end statements.","int n;\nprintf(\"Enter a number: \");\nif (scanf(\"%d\", &n) == 1) {\n    if (n % 2 == 0) puts(\"Even\");\n    else puts(\"Odd\");\n} else {\n    puts(\"Invalid input\");\n}","&n passes the address where scanf should store the integer. Checking scanf's result avoids using an uninitialised value after invalid input.","Create a menu-driven calculator using a loop. Handle invalid choices and division by zero.","Which operator gives the remainder in C?","%",["/","*"],0],
["Functions, arrays & strings","A function has a return type, name, parameters and body. Arrays store same-typed elements contiguously; their valid indices run from 0 to length-1. A C string is a char array ending with the null character \\0.","int sum(const int values[], int count) {\n    int total = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) total += values[i];\n    return total;\n}","The array parameter is accompanied by count because C does not automatically pass an array's length. const says the function will not modify the elements through this parameter.","Write functions for maximum, minimum and average of an integer array. Check what happens with an empty array.","What marks the end of a C string?","The null character \\0",["A newline","A space"],0],
["Pointers, memory & structures","A pointer stores an address. & obtains an object's address and * dereferences a pointer. Every pointer must be valid before dereferencing. malloc allocates dynamic memory and free releases it. A struct groups related fields.","int value = 42;\nint *ptr = &value;\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", *ptr);\n\nstruct Student { int id; char grade; };","ptr points to value; *ptr accesses the value. Uninitialised, dangling or out-of-bounds pointers cause undefined behaviour. Check allocation results and free owned memory exactly once.","Create a struct for a student and an array of students. Add a function to find a student by ID. Draw a memory diagram for one pointer.","What does *ptr do in an expression?","Accesses the object pointed to by ptr",["Gets the size of ptr","Frees memory"],0],
["Files, debugging & final project","Use fopen, fgets/fprintf and fclose for file work; check errors. Compile with warnings enabled, for example gcc -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic. Use a debugger and sanitizers where available. Keep functions small and document assumptions.","FILE *file = fopen(\"notes.txt\", \"w\");\nif (file != NULL) {\n    fprintf(file, \"C practice\\n\");\n    fclose(file);\n}","File operations can fail because of permissions, missing directories or storage issues. Always check return values and close files you successfully opened.","Capstone: build a contact manager that stores names and phone labels in a file. Include add, list, search, validation, error handling and a README. Never store real private contact data in a public demo.","Why compile with warnings enabled?","To detect suspicious code and mistakes early",["To make all programs secure automatically","To replace testing"],0]
]},
cpp:{name:"C++: Fundamentals to Object-Oriented Programming",intro:"Learn modern C++ fundamentals, object-oriented design, standard library containers and safer resource management.",modules:[
["First program & compilation","C++ is a compiled language with a rich standard library. A compiler translates source into an executable. Learn to compile with a modern standard such as C++20 when supported.","#include <iostream>\nint main() {\n    std::cout << \"Hello, World!\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}","iostream provides stream input/output. std::cout writes to the console; << sends values to the stream. main is the entry point.","Print a short introduction and compile it with warnings enabled.","What does std::cout do?","Writes output to the standard output stream",["Reads a file","Allocates memory"],0],
["Types, variables & expressions","C++ offers built-in types such as int, double, char and bool. Use const for values that should not change and auto when the type is clear from the initializer. Prefer initialisation with braces where appropriate.","int count{5};\ndouble rate{2.5};\nconst int limit{100};\nauto next = count + 1;","C++ is statically typed. auto deduces a type at compile time; it does not make the variable dynamically typed.","Create a unit converter with clearly named variables, constants and formatted output.","What does const communicate?","The value cannot be modified through that name",["The variable is global","The type is unknown"],0],
["Conditions, loops & functions","if/else and switch select branches. for, range-based for and while repeat work. Functions support reuse; pass small values by value and larger objects by const reference when appropriate.","int square(int n) { return n * n; }\nfor (int n : {1, 2, 3, 4}) {\n    std::cout << square(n) << '\\n';\n}","A range-based for visits each element in the initializer list. The function receives an int by value and returns its square.","Write reusable functions for a calculator and validate the user's menu selection.","What does a function return type of void mean?","It returns no value",["It returns zero","It can never be called"],0],
["Classes, objects & constructors","A class combines data and functions. public members form the interface; private members protect internal state. A constructor establishes a valid object. Use const member functions when they do not modify the object.","class Counter {\n    int value{0};\npublic:\n    void increment() { ++value; }\n    int get() const { return value; }\n};","The value is private, so callers use the public methods. This helps keep an object's state valid.","Create a BankAccount class with a private balance, deposit and withdraw methods, and validation.","Why make a data member private?","To protect invariants and control access",["To make it global","To stop the object being created"],0],
["References, STL & resource safety","References provide aliases to existing objects. std::vector is a dynamic array; std::string manages text; map and unordered_map store key-value data. Prefer standard containers and RAII to manual new/delete.","#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\nstd::vector<int> scores{70, 80, 90};\nint total = std::accumulate(scores.begin(), scores.end(), 0);","The vector manages its storage automatically. RAII ties resource lifetime to object lifetime, helping avoid leaks and premature releases.","Store expenses in a vector of structs. Use algorithms to compute totals and find the largest expense.","Which is usually safer for a growable sequence?","std::vector",["A raw pointer with manual allocation","A fixed-size unbounded array"],0],
["Files, testing & capstone","Use streams such as ifstream and ofstream for files. Separate program logic from input/output so functions can be tested. Compile with warnings, write tests and use version control.","#include <fstream>\nstd::ofstream out(\"report.txt\");\nif (out) out << \"Practice complete\\n\";","Streams manage file interaction through C++ abstractions. Check stream state after opening and after important operations.","Capstone: build a command-line library tracker using classes, vectors, file persistence, search, validation and tests. Document compile steps and limitations.","What does RAII help manage?","Resource lifetime such as memory and file handles",["Variable names","Only screen colours"],0]
]},
javascript:{name:"JavaScript: Beginner to Browser Applications",intro:"Learn the language, then use it to make interactive web pages, work with APIs and build a complete browser project.",modules:[
["Hello JavaScript & the browser","JavaScript is a programming language used in browsers and server environments. In a web page, a script can run after the document is parsed. Open browser developer tools to inspect output and errors.","console.log('Hello, World!');\nconsole.log('I am learning JavaScript.');","console.log writes a message to the developer console. JavaScript statements can be placed in a script element or a separate .js file.","Create an HTML page that loads app.js. Print a welcome message and inspect it in the browser console.","Where can JavaScript run?","In browsers and server-side runtimes",["Only inside spreadsheets","Only on mobile phones"],0],
["Variables, types & operators","Use const for bindings you do not reassign and let for bindings that change. JavaScript has number, string, boolean, bigint, symbol, undefined and object values. Use === for strict equality.","const learner = 'Sam';\nlet lessonsDone = 0;\nlessonsDone += 1;\nconsole.log(learner, lessonsDone);","const prevents reassignment of the binding, not mutation of an object. Prefer strict equality to avoid surprising type coercion.","Build a tip calculator. Convert input strings to numbers, validate them and format the result.","Which equality operator avoids type coercion?","===",["=","=="],0],
["Conditions, loops & functions","if/else handles decisions. for and while repeat tasks. Functions can be declared or assigned to variables; arrow functions provide concise syntax. Scope determines where a binding is accessible.","const square = n => n * n;\nfor (let n = 1; n <= 4; n++) {\n  console.log(square(n));\n}","The arrow function receives n and returns n*n. The loop runs four times; let keeps the counter scoped to the loop.","Write a quiz score calculator using functions, loops and clear input validation.","What does return do in a function?","Sends a value back to the caller",["Prints automatically","Stops the browser"],0],
["Arrays, objects & modern syntax","Arrays store ordered values; objects store named properties. Learn map, filter, find, reduce, destructuring, spread syntax and template literals. Choose immutable transformations when they make data flow clearer.","const expenses = [{category:'Food',amount:120},{category:'Travel',amount:50}];\nconst total = expenses.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.amount, 0);\nconsole.log(total);","reduce combines array elements into one result. Here the accumulator is the running total.","Create an array of task objects. Add functions to add, complete, filter and summarise tasks.","Which method creates a new array by transforming each element?","map()",["push()","findIndex()"],0],
["DOM, events & forms","The DOM represents a web page as objects. querySelector finds elements; textContent changes text safely; addEventListener responds to events. Validate form data and provide accessible labels and feedback.","const button = document.querySelector('#hello');\nbutton.addEventListener('click', () => {\n  document.querySelector('#output').textContent = 'Hello!';\n});","The event listener runs only after the user clicks the button. textContent treats content as text rather than parsing HTML.","Build a to-do list with add, complete and delete actions. Use labels, keyboard access and helpful empty-state messages.","Which method registers an event handler?","addEventListener()",["querySelectorAll()","innerHTML()"],0],
["Async JavaScript, APIs & modules","Promises represent work that may finish later. async/await makes promise-based code easier to read. fetch requests resources; check response.ok and handle network errors. Modules split code using export and import.","async function loadUsers() {\n  try {\n    const response = await fetch('/users.json');\n    if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Request failed');\n    const users = await response.json();\n    console.log(users);\n  } catch (error) { console.error(error); }\n}","await pauses the async function until the promise settles. Production code should also consider loading states, retries and safe handling of external data.","Capstone: build a responsive expense tracker with DOM forms, filtering, local storage, validation and a clear README. Use fictional data only.","Why check response.ok after fetch?","HTTP error statuses do not automatically reject the fetch promise",["To turn JSON into HTML","To enable JavaScript"],0]
]},
web:{name:"HTML & CSS: Build Responsive Websites",intro:"Learn the structure and presentation of the web, from semantic HTML to responsive, accessible layouts.",modules:[
["How the web page is structured","HTML describes document structure and meaning. A page has a doctype, html, head and body. The browser parses HTML into a document tree called the DOM.","<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><title>My page</title></head>\n<body><h1>Hello, World!</h1><p>My first page.</p></body>\n</html>","The head contains metadata and linked resources; the body contains visible content. The lang attribute helps assistive technologies identify the page language.","Create a personal introduction page with a title, heading, paragraphs and a meaningful language attribute.","Which element contains visible page content?","body",["head","meta"],0],
["Semantic HTML, links & images","Use elements according to their meaning: header, nav, main, section, article, aside and footer. Links navigate; buttons perform actions. Images need useful alternative text when informative.","<main>\n  <h1>My projects</h1>\n  <article><h2>Portfolio</h2><p>My work.</p></article>\n  <a href=\"projects.html\">View projects</a>\n</main>","Semantic elements communicate page structure. Use descriptive link text and alt text; decorative images can use empty alt text.","Build a portfolio page with navigation, project cards and meaningful headings. Check heading order.","Which element should wrap the page's primary unique content?","main",["span","b"],0],
["Forms & accessible controls","Forms collect input. Use label elements connected to controls, appropriate input types, required constraints and clear error messages. Native controls improve keyboard and assistive-technology support.","<form>\n <label for=\"email\">Email</label>\n <input id=\"email\" name=\"email\" type=\"email\" required>\n <button type=\"submit\">Join</button>\n</form>","A label gives a control an accessible name. Browser validation is helpful but server-side validation is still required for real applications.","Create a contact form with name, email, topic and message. Ensure every field has a label and visible focus.","How do you associate a label with an input?","Use matching for and id values",["Put the label after the page","Use placeholder only"],0],
["CSS selectors, cascade & box model","CSS rules match elements using selectors. The cascade resolves competing declarations. The box model includes content, padding, border and margin. box-sizing:border-box makes sizing more predictable.","* { box-sizing: border-box; }\n.card { padding: 1rem; border: 1px solid #ccc; margin: 1rem 0; }\n.card h2 { color: rebeccapurple; }","A class selector starts with a dot. Descendant selectors target matching elements inside another element. Specificity and source order affect the cascade.","Style your portfolio with reusable classes, spacing, typography, borders and a consistent colour system.","What are the four main box-model areas?","Content, padding, border and margin",["Header, body, footer, nav","Width, height, colour, font"],0],
["Flexbox, Grid & responsive layout","Flexbox arranges items along one main axis. CSS Grid handles rows and columns. Media queries adapt layouts to viewport size. Start with a flexible layout and add breakpoints where content needs them.",".cards { display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(220px,1fr)); gap:1rem; }\nimg { max-width:100%; height:auto; }","auto-fit and minmax create a responsive grid without hardcoding every screen size. Test narrow screens and zoom.","Build a responsive project gallery with Grid, a flexible header and readable mobile spacing.","Which layout system is especially useful for two-dimensional rows and columns?","CSS Grid",["Only inline styles","HTML tables for all layouts"],0],
["Accessibility, polish & publish","Accessibility includes keyboard use, contrast, visible focus, semantic structure, labels and reduced-motion considerations. Test with keyboard navigation and browser tools. Optimise images and keep CSS organised.",":focus-visible { outline:3px solid #7c6cff; outline-offset:3px; }\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  *, *::before, *::after { scroll-behavior:auto !important; animation-duration:0.01ms !important; }\n}","Accessible design benefits many users. A page should remain usable without a mouse and at increased text zoom.","Capstone: publish a responsive multi-page portfolio with accessible navigation, contact form UI, project cards and a clear README. Test keyboard-only use.","Why provide a visible focus style?","So keyboard users can see which control is active",["To decorate headings only","To replace labels"],0]
]},
sql:{name:"SQL: Databases from Zero",intro:"Understand relational data and write queries safely, from your first SELECT to joins, aggregation and a small database project.",modules:[
["What is a database?","A database stores organised information. A relational database stores data in tables made of rows and columns. SQL is a language for defining, querying and changing relational data. SQLite is a convenient local learning database.","CREATE TABLE students (\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  name TEXT NOT NULL,\n  year INTEGER\n);","A primary key uniquely identifies a row. NOT NULL requires a value. Column types and constraints help keep data consistent.","Design a table for books with an ID, title, author and publication year. Identify which columns should be required.","What uniquely identifies a row in a relational table?","A primary key",["A colour","A file name"],0],
["SELECT, WHERE & ORDER BY","SELECT chooses columns. FROM names the table. WHERE filters rows. ORDER BY sorts results. LIMIT restricts how many rows are returned. Use explicit columns rather than SELECT * in stable application queries.","SELECT name, year\nFROM students\nWHERE year >= 2\nORDER BY name ASC\nLIMIT 10;","SQL clauses have a logical processing order; write readable queries with clear filters. Text values use single quotes.","Create a sample products table and query items above a chosen price, sorted from highest to lowest.","Which clause filters rows?","WHERE",["ORDER BY","CREATE"],0],
["INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE & transactions","INSERT adds rows, UPDATE changes existing rows and DELETE removes rows. Always preview the target rows with SELECT before a destructive change. Transactions group changes so they can commit or roll back together.","BEGIN;\nUPDATE students SET year = 3 WHERE id = 7;\nCOMMIT;","A missing or incorrect WHERE clause can change every row. Use transactions and backups for important data.","Insert sample records, update one selected record and delete one selected record. Explain why a WHERE clause matters.","What can happen if UPDATE has no WHERE clause?","Every row may be updated",["Only the first row changes","The database closes"],0],
["Functions, NULL & aggregation","SQL includes COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN and MAX. GROUP BY forms groups; HAVING filters groups after aggregation. NULL means unknown or missing and should be tested with IS NULL, not = NULL.","SELECT year, COUNT(*) AS students\nFROM students\nGROUP BY year\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 2;","WHERE filters rows before grouping; HAVING filters groups. Aggregate functions summarise sets of rows.","Calculate total orders per customer and average order value by month using a sample dataset.","Which clause filters aggregated groups?","HAVING",["WHERE only","LIMIT"],0],
["JOINs, keys & relational design","JOIN combines related rows. INNER JOIN returns matching pairs; LEFT JOIN keeps all rows from the left table and matches where available. Foreign keys express relationships. Normalisation reduces avoidable duplication.","SELECT orders.id, customers.name\nFROM orders\nJOIN customers ON orders.customer_id = customers.id;","The join condition connects a foreign key to the referenced primary key. Incorrect join conditions can multiply rows unexpectedly.","Create customers and orders tables. Query each order with the customer's name, then find customers with no orders using LEFT JOIN.","What does LEFT JOIN preserve?","All rows from the left table",["Only rows with a match","Only the newest row"],0],
["Safe SQL, indexes & project","Use parameterised queries in applications; never concatenate untrusted input into SQL. Indexes can speed up reads but add storage and write costs. Use EXPLAIN to inspect query plans. Back up important data and apply least privilege.","-- Parameter placeholder style varies by database driver\nSELECT id, name FROM students WHERE id = ?;","The placeholder keeps user-supplied values separate from SQL syntax. Driver APIs bind the value safely.","Capstone: build a small course enrolment database with students, courses and enrolments. Include schema, sample data, joins, summary queries and a README.","Why use parameterised queries?","To prevent input from being treated as SQL syntax",["To automatically encrypt all data","To avoid database design"],0]
]},
finance:{name:"Personal Finance: Foundations & Decision Skills",intro:"Learn to organise money, understand financial trade-offs and evaluate risk. Educational material only; not personalised investment advice.",modules:[
["Money map & cash flow","Personal finance begins with knowing money coming in, money going out and the timing of both. Separate needs, wants, fixed costs, variable costs and irregular expenses. Track actual spending before setting targets.","Monthly surplus = total income - total expenses\nSavings rate = amount saved / take-home income × 100%","A budget is a plan, not a judgement. Use realistic categories and review them regularly. Do not share account numbers, passwords or private statements in public projects.","Create a fictional monthly budget with income, essentials, discretionary spending and a savings goal.","What does a cash-flow budget compare?","Money received and money spent over time",["Only investment returns","Only account balance"],0],
["Banking, interest & inflation","Understand savings accounts, deposits, fees, payment methods and statements. Simple interest is calculated on principal; compound interest earns interest on prior interest. Inflation reduces purchasing power over time.","Compound value = principal × (1 + rate / periods)^(periods × time)","Rates, fees, tax treatment and deposit protections vary by country and product. Compare effective returns after fees and applicable taxes, not only advertised rates.","Use a spreadsheet to compare fictional savings scenarios with different rates, time periods and compounding frequencies.","What is inflation?","A general rise in prices that reduces purchasing power",["A guaranteed investment return","A bank transfer"],0],
["Emergency funds, debt & credit","An emergency fund helps manage unexpected costs without relying on expensive borrowing. Compare debt by interest rate, fees, repayment schedule and consequences of missed payments. Credit reports and lending rules vary by jurisdiction.","Debt payoff plan: list balances, interest rates, minimum payments and due dates. Compare highest-interest-first and smallest-balance-first approaches.","Do not borrow to invest or treat credit limits as income. Contact lenders early if repayment becomes difficult and verify local consumer protections.","Create a fictional debt repayment table. Compare two repayment orders and explain the interest-versus-motivation trade-off.","Why build an emergency fund?","To handle unexpected costs without high-cost borrowing",["To guarantee investment profits","To avoid tracking spending"],0],
["Insurance & risk management","Insurance transfers specified financial risks in exchange for premiums, subject to exclusions, limits and deductibles. Understand what is covered, waiting periods, claims process and renewal terms. Avoid buying based only on marketing.","Expected loss (simplified) = probability of event × financial impact","Insurance is about managing potentially large losses, not making a profit. Read policy wording and verify insurer and intermediary credentials in your jurisdiction.","Compare two fictional health or vehicle policy summaries. List coverage, exclusions, limits, premium and questions to ask.","What should you review before buying insurance?","Coverage, exclusions, limits, costs and claims terms",["Only the advertisement","Only the brand colour"],0],
["Investing basics & diversification","Investing involves risk. Understand asset classes, time horizon, liquidity, volatility, diversification, fees and taxes. Equity, debt, cash-like products, gold and property behave differently. Past performance does not guarantee future results.","Real return (approx.) = nominal return - inflation rate","Risk tolerance and ability to take risk are not the same. Avoid concentrated bets, leverage and products you cannot explain. Verify current regulations and tax rules using official sources.","Build a fictional diversified portfolio comparison. Explain how its value might change in a market decline; do not use real money.","What does diversification aim to reduce?","Concentration risk",["All market risk","All possibility of loss"],0],
["Financial plan & decision project","A useful financial plan connects goals, time horizon, cash needs, risk and review dates. Keep records secure, use trusted institutions and understand fees. Never share OTPs, passwords, seed phrases or remote-access permissions.","Goal worksheet: goal · target amount · date · monthly contribution · assumptions · risks · review date","A calculator is only as good as its assumptions. Show uncertainty with scenarios and avoid promising returns. For complex tax, legal or investment decisions, consult a qualified professional.","Capstone: create a fictional personal finance dashboard with a budget, savings goals, debt schedule, insurance checklist and scenario calculator. Use sample numbers only.","What should a financial projection show?","Assumptions, scenarios, risks and limitations",["A guaranteed future balance","Only the best-case result"],0]
]}
 ,
java:{name:"Java: Programming & Object-Oriented Foundations",intro:"Learn Java from your first class through types, control flow, collections, OOP, exceptions and a practical application.",modules:[
["First Java program","Java is a statically typed, object-oriented language. Source files are compiled to bytecode and run on the Java Virtual Machine (JVM). A JDK includes tools to compile and run Java programs.","public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"Hello, World!\");\n    }\n}","The public class name must match the file name in the usual file-based workflow. main is the entry point; println prints a line.","Create Main.java that prints a short introduction and compile and run it using the JDK.","What executes Java bytecode?","The Java Virtual Machine",["A CSS browser rule","A SQL table"],0],
["Types, variables & control flow","Java has primitive types such as int, double, boolean and char, plus reference types such as String. Use if/else, switch, for and while to control program flow.","int age = 21;\nString name = \"Mira\";\nif (age >= 18) {\n    System.out.println(name + \" is an adult\");\n}","Java checks types at compile time. String is a class, not a primitive type. Use == carefully: for String content comparison, use equals().","Build a grade calculator using numeric marks, conditions and input validation.","How should you compare String contents?","equals()",["== always","compare using +"],0],
["Methods, arrays & collections","Methods organise behaviour and can accept parameters and return values. Arrays have a fixed length. ArrayList grows dynamically; choose a collection based on the problem.","static int sum(int[] values) {\n    int total = 0;\n    for (int value : values) total += value;\n    return total;\n}","The enhanced for loop visits each array element. A method's signature includes its name and parameter types.","Create methods for max, min and average, then store a list of names in an ArrayList.","What is a key difference between an array and ArrayList?","ArrayList can grow or shrink dynamically",["ArrayList cannot store objects","Arrays have no indexes"],0],
["Classes, objects & encapsulation","A class defines fields and methods. Constructors initialise objects. Access modifiers such as private and public define the interface. Encapsulation protects valid object state.","class BankAccount {\n    private double balance;\n    public void deposit(double amount) {\n        if (amount > 0) balance += amount;\n    }\n}","Keep fields private and expose methods that validate changes. For real financial software, use decimal-safe representations rather than double for currency.","Create a Student class with private fields, constructor, getters and a method to calculate an average.","Why make fields private?","To control access and protect object invariants",["To make them static","To prevent constructors"],0],
["Exceptions, files & project","Exceptions represent exceptional conditions. Use try/catch for expected failures and close resources with try-with-resources. Split code into classes with clear responsibilities and write tests.","try (var reader = new java.io.BufferedReader(new java.io.FileReader(\"notes.txt\"))) {\n    System.out.println(reader.readLine());\n} catch (java.io.IOException e) {\n    System.out.println(\"Could not read file\");\n}","Try-with-resources closes the reader automatically. Catch specific exceptions and avoid silently ignoring errors.","Capstone: build a library catalogue with classes, ArrayList, search, file persistence, validation and tests. Document how to compile and run.","What does try-with-resources provide?","Automatic closing of supported resources",["Automatic database design","Automatic bug-free code"],0]
]},
typescript:{name:"TypeScript: Safer JavaScript Applications",intro:"Learn how TypeScript adds a static type system to JavaScript and how to use it in maintainable web projects.",modules:[
["Why TypeScript?","TypeScript is a typed superset of JavaScript. The compiler checks types and emits JavaScript; browsers run the emitted JavaScript, not TypeScript directly. Start by understanding JavaScript fundamentals.","type Learner = { name: string; lessons: number };\nconst user: Learner = { name: \"Asha\", lessons: 3 };\nconsole.log(user.name);","The type describes the expected object shape. A type error can be caught before runtime, but types do not validate untrusted data received from a network.","Install TypeScript in a small project, compile one file and fix an intentional type error.","What does the TypeScript compiler output?","JavaScript",["Machine code for every browser","HTML only"],0],
["Primitive, union & inferred types","TypeScript can infer many types from initial values. Use string, number, boolean, arrays and union types. A union expresses that a value may be one of several types; narrow it before using type-specific operations.","let score = 10;\nlet status: 'new' | 'done' = 'new';\nstatus = 'done';","Literal unions constrain a variable to specific values. Prefer inference when it is clear and explicit annotations when they improve interfaces.","Model a task status using a union and write a function that handles each status.","What is a union type?","A value that may be one of several declared types",["A database join","A CSS selector"],0],
["Functions, interfaces & generics","Annotate function parameters and return types when useful. Interfaces and type aliases describe structures. Generics allow reusable functions and containers while preserving type information.","function first<T>(items: T[]): T | undefined {\n  return items[0];\n}\nconst name = first(['Asha', 'Mira']);","T is a type parameter inferred from the argument. The return type says the array might be empty.","Write a generic function that returns the last item or undefined. Define an interface for a course record.","Why use a generic type parameter?","To reuse code while preserving type information",["To skip runtime testing","To change CSS"],0],
["Narrowing, modules & async","Use typeof, in, discriminated unions and guards to narrow types. Modules use import/export. Promise and async/await types help represent asynchronous results. Treat external JSON as unknown until validated.","type Result = { ok: true; value: string } | { ok: false; error: string };\nfunction show(r: Result) {\n  if (r.ok) return r.value;\n  return r.error;\n}","The ok property discriminates the union. TypeScript narrows the object inside each branch.","Create a typed API response model and a function that handles success and error states.","What is type narrowing?","Refining a broad type to a more specific type using checks",["Changing a variable name","Compressing JavaScript"],0],
["Project setup, tests & capstone","Use strict compiler settings, linting, tests and small modules. Types improve developer feedback but do not replace runtime validation, security checks or tests. Avoid any when unknown is more appropriate.","// Example strict input guard\nfunction isString(value: unknown): value is string {\n  return typeof value === 'string';\n}","unknown forces you to check before use; any disables many type checks. Validate data at application boundaries.","Capstone: build a typed task manager with models, filters, local persistence, tests and a README explaining the type design.","Do TypeScript types automatically validate API JSON at runtime?","No; runtime validation is still needed",["Yes, always","Only when using interfaces"],0]
]},
data:{name:"Data Science Foundations: Python, Statistics & Insight",intro:"Learn how to ask data questions, inspect datasets, use Python tools, understand statistics and communicate findings responsibly.",modules:[
["Questions, data & workflow","Data science combines domain questions, data collection, cleaning, analysis and communication. Start with a question and define what a useful answer would look like. Understand where data came from and what it omits.","import csv\nwith open('sample.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    rows = list(csv.DictReader(f))\nprint('Rows:', len(rows))","A dataset is not automatically representative or accurate. Inspect column meanings, units, missing values and collection methods before drawing conclusions.","Choose a public or fictional dataset. Write three answerable questions and describe possible bias or missing data.","What should guide a data analysis?","A clear question and understanding of data limitations",["A chart colour","The largest spreadsheet"],0],
["Python for data work","Use Python variables, functions, lists and dictionaries to clean and inspect small datasets. Learn notebooks for exploration, but move reusable logic into scripts or modules when a project grows.","values = [12, 15, 18, 15, 20]\nmean = sum(values) / len(values)\nprint('Mean:', mean)","This computes a simple arithmetic mean. For larger datasets, libraries such as pandas and NumPy provide efficient tools; learn their official documentation.","Load a CSV, count rows, inspect columns and calculate a simple summary without changing the source file.","Why keep the original raw dataset unchanged?","To preserve provenance and allow reproducible cleaning",["To prevent any analysis","To make charts colourful"],0],
["Statistics: centre & spread","Mean, median and mode describe centre in different ways. Range, variance and standard deviation describe spread. Outliers can pull the mean; median can be more robust for skewed data.","data = [10, 11, 12, 13, 100]\nordered = sorted(data)\nmedian = ordered[len(ordered)//2]\nprint('Median:', median)","The extreme value 100 affects the mean much more than the median. Choose summary statistics that suit the distribution and question.","Compare mean and median for a skewed sample and explain which better represents a typical observation.","Which measure is often more robust to extreme outliers?","Median",["Mean always","Maximum"],0],
["Visualisation & communication","Choose charts based on the question: line charts for change over time, bars for category comparison, scatter plots for relationships and histograms for distributions. Label units and show relevant context.","import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nmonths = ['Jan', 'Feb', 'Mar']\nvalues = [10, 14, 12]\nplt.plot(months, values, marker='o')\nplt.ylabel('Units')\nplt.show()","A chart should clarify rather than exaggerate. Avoid misleading axes, missing denominators and unsupported causal claims.","Create two charts from a small dataset and write one sentence about what each chart can and cannot show.","Does correlation alone prove causation?","No",["Yes","Only in large datasets"],0],
["Data cleaning & reproducibility","Check duplicates, missingness, inconsistent units, invalid values and outliers. Document each transformation. Use a repeatable script, versioned inputs where possible and a clear environment file.","# A simple transparent cleaning step\nclean = [x for x in values if x is not None]\nprint('Removed missing:', len(values) - len(clean))","Filtering missing values is only one possible choice; the right method depends on why data is missing and what the analysis needs.","Write a cleaning log for a fictional dataset. Explain how each decision could change the result.","Why document data-cleaning decisions?","So results can be understood and reproduced",["To hide missing data","To guarantee perfect predictions"],0],
["Responsible analysis & capstone","Check privacy, consent, representativeness, uncertainty and limitations. Separate observation from interpretation. Do not publish sensitive personal data. Communicate uncertainty honestly and avoid claiming a model is fair or accurate without evaluation.","# Example: report a proportion with context\npositive = 42\ntotal = 100\nrate = positive / total\nprint(f'{rate:.0%} of this sample')","A percentage needs a denominator and context. A sample result may not generalise to a whole population.","Capstone: analyse a public non-sensitive dataset, document cleaning, calculate descriptive statistics, create charts and publish a short report with limitations.","What belongs in a responsible data report?","Methods, assumptions, uncertainty and limitations",["Only the best-looking chart","An unsupported prediction"],0]
]},
ux:{name:"UI/UX Design Foundations",intro:"Learn user-centred design, research, information architecture, wireframes, visual systems, prototyping and usability evaluation.",modules:[
["What is UX and UI?","User experience concerns the whole experience of using a product; user interface concerns the controls and visual surfaces people interact with. Start with user needs and the task, not decoration.","Problem statement template: [user] needs a way to [goal] because [insight].\nSuccess measure: [observable outcome].","A clear problem statement avoids jumping to a favourite feature. UX work involves research, design, testing and iteration.","Choose a familiar app and identify one user group, one task and one friction point.","What should guide product design decisions?","User needs and evidence about their tasks",["Only visual trends","The designer's favourite colour"],0],
["Research & problem framing","Research methods include interviews, observation, surveys and analysis of existing feedback. Ask neutral questions about behaviour and context. Avoid leading questions and do not collect unnecessary personal information.","Interview prompt: 'Tell me about the last time you tried to complete this task. What happened next?'","Past behaviour is often more informative than asking people to predict what they might do. Record themes, not unsupported assumptions.","Write five neutral interview questions for a student planning a weekly study schedule.","Why avoid leading interview questions?","They can bias the answers",["They take too much screen space","They improve sample size"],0],
["Information architecture & wireframes","Information architecture organises content and navigation. A wireframe shows hierarchy and layout before detailed styling. Use labels that match users' language and make important actions easy to find.","Page outline: Header → Primary navigation → Main task area → Supporting details → Footer.","A low-fidelity wireframe helps test structure cheaply before investing in polished visuals.","Sketch a dashboard with navigation, overview, one primary task and clear empty/loading/error states.","What is a wireframe mainly used for?","To explore structure and hierarchy early",["To prove backend security","To replace user testing"],0],
["Visual design & design systems","Visual hierarchy uses size, contrast, spacing and alignment to guide attention. A design system records reusable components, colour tokens, typography and interaction states. Consistency improves learnability.","Token example: --space-2: 8px; --radius-card: 12px; --text-primary: #172033;","Tokens make design changes consistent. Check contrast, legibility and responsive behaviour instead of relying on colour alone.","Create a small style guide with type scale, spacing, colour roles, buttons, cards and form states.","What is a design token?","A reusable named design decision such as a colour or spacing value",["A marketing slogan","A user password"],0],
["Prototyping & usability testing","A prototype simulates enough of an experience to test a question. Usability testing asks representative participants to attempt realistic tasks while you observe. Look for task completion, confusion and errors.","Test task: 'Find the due date for your next assignment.' Observe without telling the participant where to click.","Do not ask only 'Do you like it?' Watch what people actually do. Get consent and avoid collecting sensitive data.","Create a clickable prototype and test three tasks with a small set of participants. Record findings and prioritise fixes.","What is a useful usability test outcome?","Observed task success and points of friction",["Only whether users liked the colour","The number of prototype screens"],0],
["Accessibility, iteration & case study","Accessible design considers keyboard use, contrast, text size, labels, focus, motion and assistive technologies. Iterate based on evidence. A case study explains the problem, role, process, decisions, outcome and limitations.","Case study structure: Context → Problem → Research → Options → Prototype → Test findings → Iteration → Reflection.","Never invent user research or claim business impact you did not measure. Explain what you learned and what remains uncertain.","Capstone: design a student assignment planner. Publish research notes, user flows, wireframes, prototype, accessibility checks and a concise case study.","What makes a credible design case study?","Transparent process, evidence and honest outcomes",["Only polished mockups","Unverified impact claims"],0]
]},
sheets:{name:"Spreadsheets: Excel & Google Sheets Foundations",intro:"Learn spreadsheet structure, formulas, references, analysis, charts and practical workbooks for everyday and professional tasks.",modules:[
["Cells, rows, columns & clean data","A spreadsheet is a grid of cells. Keep one record per row and one field per column. Use clear headers, consistent units and separate raw data from calculations. Avoid merged cells in data tables.","A1: Date | B1: Category | C1: Amount\nA2: 2026-01-01 | B2: Food | C2: 120","Consistent tabular data is easier to filter, validate, analyse and import into other tools.","Create a monthly expense table with date, category, description and amount. Use fictional values.","What is a good basic data-table structure?","One record per row and one field per column",["A different layout for every row","Merged cells for every value"],0],
["Formulas & references","Formulas begin with =. Arithmetic operators and functions calculate results. Relative references change when copied; absolute references use dollar signs, such as $B$2. Mixed references lock one dimension.","=C2*D2\n=SUM(E2:E20)\n=$B$2*A2","SUM adds a range. Absolute references are useful for constants such as a tax rate or conversion factor used across many rows.","Build an invoice calculator with quantity, unit price, subtotal and a fixed tax-rate cell using an absolute reference.","What does $B$2 do when copied?","Keeps both column B and row 2 fixed",["Deletes the cell","Converts it to text"],0],
["Functions, logic & lookups","Learn SUM, AVERAGE, MIN, MAX, COUNT, IF, COUNTIF, SUMIF and lookup functions such as XLOOKUP where available. Check exact function availability in your spreadsheet application.","=IF(C2>=40,\"Pass\",\"Review\")\n=SUMIF(B2:B20,\"Food\",C2:C20)","IF chooses a result based on a condition. SUMIF adds values matching a criterion. Lookups connect a key to related information.","Create a marks sheet with pass/fail, subject averages and category totals. Test boundary values.","What does SUMIF do?","Sums values that meet a condition",["Counts only blank cells","Sorts the worksheet"],0],
["Sort, filter, validation & cleaning","Sort changes row order; filters show matching records. Data validation restricts entries and reduces mistakes. Remove duplicates carefully and preserve a raw-data copy. Watch for numbers stored as text and inconsistent date formats.","Validation idea: allow Category from a controlled list such as Food, Travel, Study, Bills.","Validation improves consistency but does not guarantee all data is correct. Always inspect imported data.","Clean a fictional transaction list, add category validation and create a filter for one month.","Why keep a raw-data copy?","To preserve the original and make transformations auditable",["To avoid formulas","To make files larger"],0],
["Pivot tables, charts & dashboards","Pivot tables summarise data by categories and measures. Charts communicate comparisons and trends. Dashboards should define metrics, units, date ranges and filters. Avoid clutter and misleading scales.","Example KPI: Total expenses = SUM(Amount)\nAverage transaction = AVERAGE(Amount)","A chart should answer a specific question. A dashboard is not useful if users cannot tell what the numbers mean.","Build a monthly spending dashboard with category totals, a trend chart and clearly labelled date range.","What should every dashboard metric make clear?","Definition, units and time period",["Only its colour","Only its font"],0],
["Modelling, audit & capstone","Separate inputs, calculations and outputs. Document assumptions, protect formula cells where appropriate and test scenarios. Avoid hard-coded values hidden inside formulas. Review errors and access permissions before sharing.","Scenario design: Base case · Lower-income case · Higher-cost case. Show assumptions in visible cells.","A spreadsheet model is a set of assumptions, not a prediction. Validate formulas with small test cases and independent checks.","Capstone: create a personal budget workbook with income, expenses, savings goals, category summaries, charts and scenario assumptions using sample data only.","What should a responsible financial spreadsheet show?","Inputs, assumptions, formulas and limitations",["A guaranteed outcome","Only the final total"],0]
]}
};
const slug=document.body.dataset.course;const course=C[slug];const nav=document.getElementById('moduleNav'),content=document.getElementById('lessonContent');const key='skillspring-'+slug+'-progress';let progress=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(key)||'{}');let active=Number(localStorage.getItem(key+'-active')||0);
document.title=course.name+' · SkillSpring';
document.getElementById('courseName').textContent=course.name;document.getElementById('courseIntro').textContent=course.intro;
function update(){const done=Object.values(progress).filter(Boolean).length,pct=Math.round(done/course.modules.length*100);document.getElementById('progressBar').style.width=pct+'%';document.getElementById('progressText').textContent=pct+'% complete';document.getElementById('lessonCount').textContent=done+' of '+course.modules.length+' modules completed'}
function render(){active=Math.max(0,Math.min(course.modules.length-1,active));localStorage.setItem(key+'-active',active);nav.innerHTML=course.modules.map((m,i)=>'<button class="module-link '+(i===active?'active ':'')+(progress[i]?'done':'')+'" data-i="'+i+'"><span class="num">'+(progress[i]?'✓':String(i+1).padStart(2,'0'))+'</span><span>'+m[0]+'</span></button>').join('');const m=course.modules[active];content.innerHTML='<div class="lesson"><div class="eyebrow">MODULE '+(active+1)+' OF '+course.modules.length+'</div><h2>'+m[0]+'</h2><h3>Understand the concept</h3><p>'+m[1]+'</p><h3>Worked example</h3><div class="code"><button class="copy">Copy</button><pre></pre></div><div class="callout">'+m[3]+'</div><div class="task"><h3>Practice task</h3><p>'+m[4]+'</p><p>Write your solution in your own editor or the matching practice tool. Test normal cases and edge cases. Keep your work in a GitHub repository as you progress.</p></div><div class="quiz"><h3>Knowledge check</h3><p>'+m[5]+'</p>'+m[6].map((v,i)=>'<label><input type="radio" name="answer" value="'+i+'"> '+String.fromCharCode(65+i)+'. '+v+'</label>').join('')+'<button id="check">Check answer</button><div class="feedback" id="feedback"></div></div><div class="lesson-actions"><button class="secondary" id="prev" '+(active===0?'disabled':'')+'>← Previous</button><button class="complete" id="done">'+(progress[active]?'✓ Completed · Mark incomplete':'Mark complete')+'</button><button class="primary" id="next">'+(active===course.modules.length-1?'Review course':'Next module →')+'</button></div></div>';const pre=content.querySelector('pre');pre.textContent=m[2];content.querySelector('.copy').onclick=()=>navigator.clipboard?.writeText(pre.textContent);document.getElementById('prev').onclick=()=>{active--;render()};document.getElementById('next').onclick=()=>{if(active<course.modules.length-1)active++;render();window.scrollTo(0,0)};document.getElementById('done').onclick=()=>{progress[active]=!progress[active];localStorage.setItem(key,JSON.stringify(progress));render()};document.getElementById('check').onclick=()=>{const choice=content.querySelector('input[name=answer]:checked');document.getElementById('feedback').textContent=!choice?'Choose an answer.':Number(choice.value)===m[7]?'Correct! '+m[3]:'Not quite. Review the concept and try again. '+m[3]};update()}
nav.addEventListener('click',e=>{const b=e.target.closest('[data-i]');if(b){active=Number(b.dataset.i);render()}});document.getElementById('resetProgress').onclick=()=>{if(confirm('Reset progress for this course on this device?')){progress={};active=0;localStorage.removeItem(key);render()}};render();